openfx-gmic: G'MIC filters as OpenFX plug-ins
==============================================

openfx-gmic makes the filters of G'MIC (https://gmic.eu), an open-source image processing
framework, available as OpenFX plug-ins, mainly for Natron (https://natrongithub.github.io).
A single GMIC.ofx bundle registers one plug-in per filter of the G'MIC standard library and its
community filters: 1199 plug-ins with G'MIC 4.0.8 (810 in COMMERCIAL builds). Changes since
Natron 2.5 are in CHANGELOG.md.

It was written by Tobias Fleischer (reduxFX Productions, https://www.reduxfx.com), and is now
maintained by the Natron developers at https://github.com/NatronGitHub/openfx-gmic. The original
code also built plug-ins for Adobe After Effects and Premiere Pro (AE_PLUGIN): these parts are
unmaintained, and only the OpenFX build is supported.

Licence
-------

Dual-licensed, choose one of (see COPYING):
- CeCILL-C, close to the GNU LGPL (Licence_CeCILL-C_V1-en.txt,
  http://www.cecill.info/licences/Licence_CeCILL-C_V1-en.html);
- CeCILL v2.0, compatible with the GNU GPL (Licence_CeCILL_V2-en.txt,
  http://www.cecill.info/licences/Licence_CeCILL_V2-en.html).

COMMERCIAL builds (LICENSE=COMMERCIAL), made for Natron's COMMERCIAL builds, contain no GPL
code: only code compatible with the CeCILL-C option (pocketfft is BSD-3-Clause, some filters
CeCILL-B). The default (GPL) build also includes community filters under other licences
(CeCILL-only, GPL, non-commercial: see gmic_stdlib_commercial.txt), and GPL builds may link FFTW
(GPL) instead of pocketfft (FFT=fftw3). The licence texts are installed in the bundle's
Contents/Resources. COMMERCIAL builds are no longer CI-tested, and are low priority.

Getting the sources
-------------------

    git clone --recursive https://github.com/NatronGitHub/openfx-gmic.git
    git submodule update -i    # in an existing clone: the openfx submodule is required

The G'MIC sources (version GMICVER in the Makefile, 4.0.8) are downloaded at build time and are
not in git: the release tarball gmic_<version>.tar.gz, from the GitHub release of GreycLab/gmic,
gmic.eu or the MacPorts distfiles mirror, checked against GMICSRC_SHA256_<version>. They are then
patched: patches/cimg-omp-rand.sh makes CImg's random numbers in OpenMP loops reproducible,
patches/cimg-vvar2.sh, patches/cimg-vargkth2.sh, patches/cimg-log1p-peephole.sh and
patches/cimg-abs-peephole.sh fix math parser bugs,
patches/cimg-fftw-no-cleanup-at-unload.sh keeps FFTW's threads when the plug-in is unloaded
(FFT=fftw3), patches/cimg-msvc-snprintf.sh makes cimg_snprintf() always end its output with a
null character with MSVC, patches/gmic-win-thread-stack.sh gives the threads of G'MIC's
"parallel" command an 8 MiB stack on Windows, patches/gmic-atomic-abort.sh makes G'MIC read and
write its abort flags atomically (they are shared between threads), and gmic_hotfixes.gmic replaces the
definitions of broken filters (each fix is skipped once G'MIC has it).

Requirements
------------

- A C++17 compiler (C++20 for Qt6 builds of Natron), pkg-config, and make and curl, or CMake
  >= 3.21 (with Ninja for the example below, or any other generator).
- Libraries, found through pkg-config: zlib, libpng; with FFT=fftw3 only,
  fftw3 >= 3.3.5 and fftw3_threads (the default FFT, pocketfft, is header-only and downloaded at
  build time).
- Linux: the static archives of these libraries (libz.a, libpng16.a; libfftw3.a and
  libfftw3_threads.a with FFT=fftw3), built with -fPIC, found through pkg-config. They are linked
  statically, so that GMIC.ofx needs only system libraries. Distributions' archives are often not
  built with -fPIC (e.g. Ubuntu's libpng16.a), and then fail to link: build them with
  .github/workflows/build_static_deps.sh <prefix>, as CI does, and set
  PKG_CONFIG_PATH=<prefix>/lib/pkgconfig. GMIC_STATIC_DEPS=0 (CMake: -DGMIC_STATIC_DEPS=OFF) links
  the shared zlib and libpng instead: distribution packages and Natron's
  release builds use it (Natron's installer bundles these libraries).
- Python 3, for the render test only.
- Platforms: Linux, macOS (Apple clang; libraries from MacPorts or Homebrew) and Windows: MSVC
  x64 with CMake, as in CI (see "Building with MSVC" below), or MSYS2 (UCRT64, tested weekly; with make or CMake;
  libraries from its packages: mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-zlib, -libpng, -libgomp and -pkgconf).

Runtime requirements on Linux: GMIC.ofx needs only the system libraries libc, libm, libstdc++,
libgcc_s and libgomp (OpenMP builds; libgomp is a separate package, which minimal systems may not
have), plus libpthread, libdl and librt before glibc 2.34. Natron 2.6's Linux baseline is Rocky
Linux 9 (glibc 2.34, GCC 11's libstdc++): a build there runs on newer distributions.

Downloads: the plug-in doesn't use libcurl, on any system. The filters that download data (CLUTs
of Color Presets and Simulate Film, CNN networks, sample images...) run the system's curl command:
/usr/bin/curl on macOS, curl.exe from the system directory (System32, part of Windows 10 1803 and
later; then PowerShell) on Windows, and curl, or wget if curl fails, from PATH on Linux. The
plug-in sets these paths, so that CImg doesn't look for the commands in the host's current
directory first (a PATH that contains "." or an empty entry still does). Without them, these
filters fail with "Unreachable network file". Unlike the libcurl code of earlier builds, the curl
command verifies TLS certificates: downloads may fail behind a proxy that intercepts TLS with a
certificate that the system doesn't trust.

Building with make
------------------

CI builds with CMake (https://github.com/NatronGitHub/Natron/issues/1181), as Natron's
build-plugins.sh does by default. The Makefile is a fallback (build-plugins.sh's
PLUGINS_BUILD_SYSTEM=make), tested only by one weekly Linux release build compared with the CMake
one. CMakeLists.txt reads the G'MIC version, the checksums, POCKETFFTCOMMIT, CIMG_PATCHES, GMIC_PATCHES and
GMIC_H_PATCHES from it.

    make CONFIG=release OPENMP=1 CXXSTD=c++17
    make CONFIG=release OPENMP=1 CXXSTD=c++17 install   # may need root

The bundle is GMIC_OFX/<OS>-<BITS>-<CONFIG>/GMIC.ofx.bundle (e.g. GMIC_OFX/Linux-64-release).
"make install", with the same options as the build, copies it to /usr/OFX/Plugins (macOS:
/Library/OFX/Plugins; Windows: C:\Program Files\Common Files\OFX\Plugins). "make distclean"
also removes the downloaded sources.

Options:
- CONFIG: release or relwithdebinfo (default: an unoptimised -g build in <OS>-<BITS>-debug).
- OPENMP=1: multithreaded G'MIC (off by default). On macOS, libomp is taken from LIBOMP_PREFIX
  (default /opt/local, MacPorts' layout: include/libomp and lib/libomp); with Homebrew's libomp,
  build with CMake and OFX_LIBOMP_ROOT.
- CXXSTD: e.g. c++17 or c++20 (default: the compiler's).
- GMIC_STATIC_DEPS: Linux only, 1 (default): link zlib, libpng and FFTW statically; 0: shared.
- LICENSE: GPL (default) or COMMERCIAL (see "Licence"; gmic_stdlib_commercial.txt lists the
  filters left out, and why).
- FFT: pocketfft (default, downloaded; BSD-3-Clause, as in openfx-misc) or fftw3 (FFTW, opt-in,
  GPL builds only).
- POCKETFFT_HEADER: a downloaded pocketfft_hdronly.h, for offline builds (copied, then checked
  against POCKETFFT_SHA256); without it, it is downloaded at POCKETFFTCOMMIT.

GMIC_OFX/GMIC_OFX.xcodeproj is unmaintained (it still uses FFTW and an old file list): build with
make or CMake.

Building with CMake
-------------------

    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    cmake --build build              # build/Plugins/GMIC.ofx.bundle
    cmake --install build --prefix <dir>   # <dir>/OFX/Plugins/GMIC.ofx.bundle

Distribute the installed bundle, not build/Plugins: on Linux, the build tree's GMIC.ofx has a
RUNPATH that CMake pads with ':' (empty entries, which the loader takes as the current
directory) until cmake --install rewrites it, and the build tree's bundle has no Info.plist.

Options (the Makefile's equivalent in parentheses):
- CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE: Release (default), Debug or RelWithDebInfo (CONFIG).
- OFX_CXX_STANDARD: 17 (default) or 20 (CXXSTD).
- OFX_USE_OPENMP: ON (default) (OPENMP=1); OFX_LIBOMP_ROOT: libomp for Apple clang (default:
  MacPorts'; Homebrew: -DOFX_LIBOMP_ROOT=$(brew --prefix libomp)).
- OFX_LICENSE: GPL (default) or COMMERCIAL (LICENSE); FFT: pocketfft (default) or fftw3 (FFT).
- GMIC_TARBALL: a downloaded gmic_<version>.tar.gz, for offline builds; GMIC_DOWNLOAD_DIR: where
  to download it (default: the build directory).
- GMIC_POCKETFFT: a downloaded pocketfft_hdronly.h (POCKETFFT_HEADER), for offline builds: the
  file at the Makefile's POCKETFFTCOMMIT, checked against POCKETFFT_SHA256 (downloaded there if
  missing).
- GMIC_STATIC_DEPS: Linux only, ON (default) or OFF (GMIC_STATIC_DEPS=1 or 0).
- OFX_DEPLOY_BUNDLES=ON: on macOS, the install step copies the libraries into the bundle.
  GMIC.ofx targets macOS 14.0 (CMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET, MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET), but the
  copied libraries keep the macOS version they were built for: the CI macOS artifacts bundle
  MacPorts libraries built for the runner (macOS 15), so they need macOS 15; Natron's release
  builds (its SDK) target 14.0.
- GMIC_BUILD_TESTS=ON: also build openfx's hostDemo, for the render test.
- GMIC_VCOMP_DLL: MSVC with OpenMP: the vcomp140.dll copied into the bundle (default: the
  redistributable one of the compiler, see below).

Building with MSVC
------------------

CI builds the Windows x64 bundle with Visual Studio's compiler (cl.exe), from a "x64 Native Tools"
environment (vcvars64.bat), with CMake and Ninja, from Git for Windows' bash: the patches of the
G'MIC sources need its sh and awk in PATH (or -DGMIC_PATCH_SH=<path to sh.exe>). zlib and libpng
(and expat, for the test hosts of GMIC_BUILD_TESTS) come from vcpkg, at the versions pinned by
vcpkg.json (its builtin-baseline), as static libraries with the static C runtime:

    cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=cl -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=cl -DCMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY=MultiThreaded \
      -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=<vcpkg>/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake -DVCPKG_TARGET_TRIPLET=x64-windows-static
    cmake --build build
    cmake --install build --prefix <dir>

OpenMP uses MSVC's /openmp (OpenMP 2.0): the bundle ships the compiler's redistributable
vcomp140.dll (VC/Redist/MSVC/<version>/x64/Microsoft.VC<nnn>.OpenMP) next to GMIC.ofx, and a
manifest embedded into GMIC.ofx makes the loader use that copy. GMIC.ofx then imports only
KERNEL32, SHELL32 and VCOMP140 (no C++ runtime, zlib or libpng DLL). The plug-in keeps vcomp140.dll
loaded once it has rendered (unloading it under its idle threads crashed hosts that unload
plug-ins). Debug builds link the same vcomp140.dll (vcomp140d.dll is not redistributable).
Contents/Resources/vcomp140-NOTICE.txt says that vcomp140.dll is Microsoft's redistributable, not
under the plug-in's licence. /openmp:llvm is not supported.

Windows on Arm (CI builds them weekly, natively on windows-11-arm, from an "ARM64 Native Tools"
environment, vcvarsall.bat arm64):
- ARM64: as above, with -DVCPKG_TARGET_TRIPLET=arm64-windows-static. The plug-in is installed
  into Contents/Win-arm64, a reserved folder of the OFX packaging spec, for native ARM64 hosts.
- Arm64EC: also with CFLAGS=-arm64EC CXXFLAGS=-arm64EC in the environment, and
  -DVCPKG_TARGET_TRIPLET=arm64ec-windows-static (vcpkg-triplets/, found through
  vcpkg-configuration.json): Contents/Win-arm64ec, for Arm64EC hosts (and x64 hosts running under
  emulation, which also look there).
- Arm64X, one GMIC.ofx with both, Microsoft's two-pass recipe: an ARM64 build configured with
  -DGMIC_ARM64X=ARM64 -DGMIC_ARM64X_RSP=<file> writes its linker inputs into <file> (MSVC 17.11
  or later); an Arm64EC build configured afterwards with -DGMIC_ARM64X=ARM64EC and the same
  GMIC_ARM64X_RSP links them into an Arm64X GMIC.ofx, installed into Contents/Win-arm64ec.
OpenMP works on both (the ARM64 Redist vcomp140.dll is an Arm64X image, bundled as on x64).
The weekly CI artifact openfx-gmic-windows-msvc is one bundle for x64, Arm64EC and ARM64 hosts:
Contents/Win64 (x64), Contents/Win-arm64ec (the Arm64X GMIC.ofx) and Contents/Win-arm64 (the
ARM64 one), each with its vcomp140.dll. It is only made when both Windows jobs succeed, so after
a failed week there is no fresh Windows download until the next green weekly or on-demand run;
the previous bundle stays available for up to 14 days. The weekly run is never skipped: the
toolchains (package managers, runner images) move even when master doesn't.
Arm64EC with a CMake that doesn't report ARM64EC as the compiler's architecture: -arm64EC (or
/arm64EC) in CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS is enough for the choice of vcomp140.dll.

Filters
-------

The filters come from the first of these that is not empty:
- ~/.config/gmic/gmic_ofx.gmic (Windows: %APPDATA%/gmic/gmic_ofx.gmic);
- ~/.config/gmic/gmic_stdlib.gmic (Windows: %APPDATA%/gmic/gmic_stdlib.gmic);
- the standard library built into the plug-in.
A user file replaces the whole filter list. Plug-in identifiers come from the filter names.

Environment
-----------

- OFX_GMIC_FRAME_CACHE_MB: memory budget, in MB, of the cache of Output Layer "Frame" results
  (default: 1/8 of the physical memory, between 1 and 4 GiB; 0 disables the cache).
  A decimal number >= 0 ("512", "0.5", "2e3"; '.' as decimal point whatever the locale,
  surrounding whitespace allowed): a sign, inf, nan, hexadecimal, other text or an overflow give
  the default, with a note on stderr.

Tests
-----

There are no unit tests. The CI scripts can be run locally:
- .github/workflows/describe_plugins.sh <dir with GMIC.ofx.bundle> <minimum plug-in count>
  builds a minimal OpenFX host and describes every plug-in.
- .github/workflows/render_plugins.py renders the plug-ins with openfx's hostDemo (CMake with
  -DGMIC_BUILD_TESTS=ON); see the script's help.

Contact
-------

Report bugs at https://github.com/NatronGitHub/openfx-gmic/issues. Bugs in the filters
themselves belong upstream: https://github.com/GreycLab/gmic (G'MIC and its standard library)
or https://github.com/GreycLab/gmic-community (community filters).
